CH = dict(num=11, title="From Barter to Money", tag="Economics", theme="Barter, functions of money, coins, paper and digital money",
 intro="Before money, people swapped goods directly. As trade grew, the difficulties of swapping pushed societies towards a common medium of exchange. This chapter traces barter, the problems that led to money, the functions money performs, and its journey from cowrie shells and coins to notes and UPI.",
 big_questions=["How did exchange take place before money?", "Why did money come into existence?", "How has money changed form over time?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "11.1 The Barter System"),
 ("ul", ["**Barter**: exchanging goods and services for other goods and services without using money (e.g. an extra eraser for a classmate's extra pencil). It is the earliest form of exchange, with evidence from all over the world.",
  "Commodities used as a medium: cowrie shells, salt, tea, tobacco, cloth, cattle (cows, goats, horses, sheep), seeds. Unusual examples: **Rai stones** (giant stone discs, Yap Island, Micronesia), the **Aztec Tajadero** (copper, Mexico), **Tevau** feather coils (Solomon Islands).",
  "**Money** = common tool accepted by everyone in exchange for goods and services. A **transaction** is an act of buying or selling; **commodities** are goods that can be bought and sold."]),
 ("h2", "11.2 Why Do We Need Money? Problems of Barter"),
 ("p", "Think of a farmer with only an ox who needs shoes, a sweater and medicine. He cannot easily swap one ox for many things."),
 ("table", ["Problem", "Meaning", "Farmer's example"], [
  ["Double coincidence of wants", "Both parties must want exactly what the other offers", "Finding someone who needs an ox and offers shoes, sweater and medicine"],
  ["No common measure of value", "No agreed way to compare the worth of different goods", "How much wheat equals a sweater?"],
  ["Lack of divisibility", "Goods cannot always be split into parts", "Cannot exchange only part of an ox"],
  ["Lack of portability", "Hard to carry goods from place to place", "Taking the ox everywhere"],
  ["Lack of durability", "Goods perish and cannot be stored", "Wheat rots or is eaten by rats"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Divisibility**: can be split into portions. **Portability**: can be carried easily. **Durability**: lasts long without damage. Good money is accepted by all, divisible, portable and durable."),
 ("h3", "Barter that survives today"),
 ("ul", ["**Junbeel Mela**, Morigaon district, Assam (*jun* = moon, *beel* = wetland): three-day fair that began with a gathering of Tiwa, Karbi, Khasi and Jaintia chiefs from the **15th century**. It opens with **Agni Puja**. Hill people barter roots, vegetables, fruits, herbs, spices and forest handmade goods for rice cakes and other foods from the plains.",
  "Other examples: book swap clubs; exchanging **old clothes for new utensils** with a door-to-door vendor (household clears items; vendor resells or recycles)."]),
 ("h2", "11.3 Functions of Money"),
 ("table", ["Function", "Explanation"], [
  ["Medium of exchange", "Accepted by everyone as payment for goods and services, removing the double-coincidence problem"],
  ["Store of value", "Can be saved and used later without rotting (the farmer keeps money, not wheat)"],
  ["Unit of account / common denomination", "Measures and compares the value of goods and services through prices"],
  ["Standard of deferred payment", "Allows payment later (e.g. paying the remaining amount for a book at a later date)"]]),
 ("p", "Money circulates: parents pay shopkeepers, shopkeepers pay workers' salaries, workers buy goods and pay school fees."),
 ("h2", "11.4 The Journey of Money"),
 ("table", ["Stage", "Approximate period / details"], [
  ["Barter and commodity money", "Before about 1000 BCE; cowrie shells (around 6000 BCE onward)"],
  ["Metal coinage", "From about 600 BCE; iron, silver, gold, copper"],
  ["Official paper money", "Introduced in India in the late 18th century; official paper money (1861)"],
  ["Digital money", "Debit cards, credit cards, net banking; UPI (2016)"]]),
 ("h3", "Coinage"),
 ("ul", ["Rulers issued coins; **minting** (producing coins) was controlled by rulers. Coins of powerful kings were accepted beyond their kingdoms, easing trade across regions.",
  "Made of gold, silver, copper or **alloys** (mixtures of metals). Ancient coins were called **karshapanas or panas** with punched symbols called **rupas**. 'Pana' survives in Tamil, Telugu and Malayalam as *panam* and in Kannada as *hana* (money). Today's coins are alloys largely of iron with chromium, silicon, carbon.",
  "Coin sides: **obverse** (head) and reverse carried motifs of animals, trees, hills, kings, deities. Examples: **Chalukyas of Kalyana** — Varaha (Vishnu avatar) and a three-tiered parasol; **Cholas** — tiger emblem; Roman gold coins found at **Pudukkottai** (Tamil Nadu) and in Kerala show brisk overseas trade, in India's favour.",
  "Old units: **1 anna = 1/16 rupee**; in 1947 one anna bought a dozen bananas.",
  "The **₹ symbol** was adopted in **2010**, designed by **Udaya Kumar** (IIT Bombay): a blend of Devanagari 'Ra' and Roman 'R' with two stripes (flag and 'equals' sign)."]),
 ("h3", "Paper money"),
 ("ul", ["Heavy coins were hard to carry and store for big transactions, so **paper money** came, first used in **China**, introduced in India in the **late 18th century** (Bank of Bengal and Bank of Bombay notes).",
  "Coins are used for small amounts, notes for higher **denominations** (units of coins and notes, e.g. 50 paise, ₹1, ₹10, ₹500). Notes have motifs of India's heritage on the reverse and tactile features for the visually impaired.",
  "Only the **Reserve Bank of India (RBI)** can legally issue currency; no one else may."]),
 ("h3", "New forms of money"),
 ("ul", ["**Digital money** is intangible: electronic. Methods: debit and credit cards, net banking, **UPI (Unified Payments Interface)** and **QR codes** (quick-response codes scanned by phone to pay straight into the receiver's bank account, as used by fruit seller Krishnappa).",
  "These transfer money from one bank account to another without cash."]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "Arthashastra: an annual salary of 60 panas could be replaced by an *adhaka* (about 3 kg) of grain a day; the fine for failing to help a neighbour was 100 panas — more than an annual salary, showing the value placed on community duty."),
 ("h2", "11.5 Quick Revision"),
 ("qa", [("What is barter?", "Direct exchange of goods and services without money."),
  ("Name commodities used as money.", "Cowrie shells, salt, tea, tobacco, cloth, cattle, seeds, Rai stones."),
  ("What are the problems of barter?", "Double coincidence of wants, no common measure of value, lack of divisibility, portability and durability."),
  ("What is double coincidence of wants?", "Two people each have exactly what the other wants and can exchange directly."),
  ("List the functions of money.", "Medium of exchange, store of value, unit of account and standard of deferred payment."),
  ("Where does barter survive?", "Junbeel Mela in Assam, book swaps, old clothes for utensils."),
  ("What were karshapanas?", "Ancient Indian punch-marked coins of silver, copper or alloys; symbols were called rupas."),
  ("What do Roman coins at Pudukkottai show?", "Active overseas trade of south India, in India's favour."),
  ("Why did paper money replace coins for large payments?", "Heavy coins are hard to carry and store."),
  ("Who issues currency in India?", "The Reserve Bank of India."),
  ("What is a QR code payment?", "Scanning a code with a phone to transfer money directly to the receiver's bank account."),
  ("Who designed the rupee symbol and when was it adopted?", "Udaya Kumar of IIT Bombay; adopted in 2010.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["**Barter** came first; its flaws (double coincidence, no common value, indivisible, non-portable, perishable) led to **money**.",
  "Money is a **medium of exchange, store of value, unit of account and deferred payment** standard.",
  "Money evolved: **commodities → metal coins (panas) → paper notes → digital (UPI, cards, QR)**.",
  "The **RBI** alone issues currency in India."])
